<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
        <artifactId>rains-flexible-modules</artifactId>
        <version>1.0.0</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>speedrun-standalone</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>Rain's Speedrun</name>
    <description>
        Rain's Speedrun, as a plugin of its own, Manhunt included. Nothing here but a build file and a
        descriptor: the speedrun module and the standard wrapper are shaded together, and that is the
        whole plugin. A server coming from RainsManhunt has its files moved in on the first start.
    </description>

    <properties>
        <!-- Terminal deployable plugin jar, not a Maven library: never published to the registry. -->
        <maven.deploy.skip>true</maven.deploy.skip>
        <!-- The name the server sees, and the name of its data folder. -->
        <plugin.name>RainsSpeedrun</plugin.name>

        <!-- 1.0.0: the first release of this as its own plugin — split out of RainsCore, where it
             briefly lived built in, after a join handler there cleared a player's inventory on every
             join anywhere on the server. Not a rebuild of an earlier standalone speedrun plugin.
             1.1.0: a run hazard — breaking a block, or opening a chest/container, spawns a creeper,
             occasionally charged, each with its own toggle and its own chance from the lobby menu.
             1.2.0: the on/off hazard toggles became spawn-chance percentages; /lemmemove, /starthere,
             /speedrunreset and /speedrunspectate; and every setting (not only the goal/death policy)
             the lobby menu shows can now be reached mid-run where it actually applies live.
             1.2.1: /speedrunreset now deletes and remakes the world from scratch even while READY
             with nothing running, instead of refusing — "regenerate" always meant a full wipe, not a
             revert, whatever state the world happened to be in.
             1.3.0: the lobby's own world reset/create/delete now runs on RainsCore's generic
             WorldRegenerator (requires RainsCore 1.29.1+) instead of a copy of the same logic —
             occupants are evacuated to wherever they actually were before entering the world, not a
             generic spawn. The lobby world is also created automatically on first enable if it does
             not exist yet, defaulting to a dedicated "speedrun" world rather than the server's own
             primary one, which can never be regenerated at all.
             1.4.0: /speedrun — the one way into the lobby world at all, until now. Every other entry
             point (the compass, the start block, /lemmemove, /starthere) assumed a player was already
             standing in it; nothing ever explained how they got there in the first place.
             1.5.0: every join lands in the lobby world, and the items come back the moment a reset
             finishes rather than only on the next join; each racer's respawn point is set to where
             their run began. And the fix behind the bug report: a dragon-kill run's clock is armed by
             the dragon actually dying, not by the kill advancement — an advancement is granted once
             per player, ever, so on a repeat racer's second run the exit portal ended nothing at all.
             The chosen goal is now also revoked as a run arms, so the plain advancement goals can be
             earned a second time too.
             1.6.0: a run is played across its own three worlds — <name>, <name>_nether and
             <name>_the_end are created together, portal travel is kept inside them, and a reset
             wipes all three. A nether portal used to drop a racer into the server's own nether, and
             walking back out of that left them in the server's overworld, outside the race. The
             dragon run also ends when a racer leaves the End at all, not only when the exit portal
             happens to arrive as a portal event — it did not, and the clock kept running.
             1.11.0: the lobby can play a game other than a plain race. A module installed beside
             this one offers a SpeedrunMode (Manhunt is the first), the compass's menu cycles to it,
             and that game is then played in this world, with this countdown, this clock and this
             reset — rather than in a second lobby of its own beside it.
             1.14.0: a reset with somebody still in the run's nether or end really resets all three.
             The overworld went first, and whoever was left in the nether was sent back to where they
             had entered it — the overworld that had just been deleted — which ended the reset there.
             The whole run is now one regeneration through RainsCore's WorldRegenerator.regenerateAll,
             which moves everybody out of every world first; portal linking and seeds are Core's too,
             and the module's own copies (SpeedrunReset, SpeedrunSeed) are gone. Needs RainsCore 1.36.0.
             1.15.0: a death in the run's nether or end comes back into the run. A racer killed there
             respawned at the server's own spawn — out of the race, in a world no reset ever touches,
             with the clock still running: a world made at runtime is never the primary level, so a
             player with no respawn point of their own lands in the server's. And /freezeagain
             (alias /unlemmemove) undoes a /lemmemove, which until now lasted until the world was
             reset around everybody.
             1.16.0: a reset forgets the /starthere point. Those coordinates are in the world the
             reset deletes, and the world that comes back is generated from a new seed — so the spot
             they name is somewhere else entirely, and the next countdown teleported every racer into
             it. A lobby with no start point starts everybody where they stand, which is the
             documented "off".
             1.17.0: every racer's advancements are wiped as a run starts (switchable, on by
             default). Only the goal used to be cleared, which kept the end condition working but
             still let a repeat racer start with the advancement screen already filled in — no
             toasts, no progress to chase — while the first-timer beside them played the real thing.
             And /speedrunreset now ends where a run that ended on its own does: everybody standing
             in the run's worlds is put back into the fresh lobby with the items, instead of being
             scattered wherever the evacuation dropped them.
             1.18.0: a new round carries nothing over — XP, inventory, ender chest, respawn point and
             the rest of the body are reset as a run starts. The lobby items cannot be dropped (Core's
             BoundItems). Leaving the End by dying or being teleported out after the dragon no longer
             counts as taking the exit portal. Needs RainsCore 1.37.0.
             1.19.0: before a run nobody may change the world at all — placing, buckets, chests,
             doors, trampling and item frames, not only breaking. A game mode can place everybody
             for the countdown itself (Manhunt's circle).
             1.20.0: only the exit portal itself — or the respawn after the end credits — wins a run;
             leaving the End any other way (dying, a command, the join handler) no longer does, and
             the log names who finished it. A racer reconnecting in the run's Nether or End stays there.
             1.21.0: a game mode may end its own run (SpeedrunMode.endsItself), so it can start
             without a goal.
             1.22.0: /speedrunresume [time] starts a run over the world as it stands — nobody moved,
             healed or cleared; /speedruntime sets the clock. Lobby items no longer clear an inventory.
             1.23.0: SpeedrunControl lets a mode start or resume the lobby in its own mode; refusals
             say the real reason.
             1.24.0: SpeedrunSession.addParticipant — a mode can bring a latecomer into a running run.
             A finished run can be resumed over without regenerating the world.
             1.25.0: review — a stale reset timer can no longer regenerate a later run's world; a refused resume keeps the finished run; Folia-safe start-of-run reset; no regeneration while stopping.
             1.26.0: edge cases — only the run's own End counts for the dragon, resumed runs stay winnable, world pinned for the run, quitters dropped at zero, clean disable mid-run. Needs RainsCore 1.42.0.
             1.27.0: one menu for everything, pre-flight check, setup assistant, live splits vs personal best and record, run history and leaderboards, seed control, practice kits. Needs RainsCore 1.43.0.
             1.28.0: Manhunt is built in — one plugin, one menu, one setup, one preflight, one HUD, one history; RainsManhunt's data is moved over on first start (backup kept). Security: every subcommand and button checks its own permission, player text is escaped, whitelist clear/vip need rainsmanhunt.manhunt.whitelist.admin. Needs RainsCore 1.43.0.
             1.28.1: built against RainsCore 1.44.0 (player text is inserted as plain text Core-wide). Needs RainsCore 1.44.0. -->
        <plugin.version>1.28.1</plugin.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>speedrun-module</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>modules-wrapper</artifactId>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <finalName>${plugin.name}-${plugin.version}</finalName>
        <resources>
            <resource>
                <directory>src/main/resources</directory>
                <!-- The descriptor names the version, and a version written twice is one that drifts. -->
                <filtering>true</filtering>
            </resource>
        </resources>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <!--
              StandaloneJarTest reads the jar this build produces, so it has to run after it exists.
              Surefire bound to a later phase rather than failsafe, which is one more plugin to have
              cached — this build has to work offline.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>default-test</id>
                        <phase>none</phase>
                    </execution>
                    <execution>
                        <id>after-the-jar-exists</id>
                        <phase>integration-test</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>test</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <skipTests>false</skipTests>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
